# ADSB Radarscope — moving receiver position
#
# Rewrite this file from whatever produces your fix and the scope follows it.
# It is read when the receiver type is 'car' or 'ship' — set that in
# Settings > Receiver, or as RECEIVER_TYPE in config.js. Blank lines and lines starting with # are ignored.
#
# Four formats are accepted. Use whichever your GPS source already emits:
#
#   1. Bare pair          -22.878029, -43.155934
#                         -22.878029 -43.155934 180 3.0    (lat lon course speed)
#
#   2. KEY=VALUE          LAT=-22.878029
#                         LON=-43.155934
#                         HEADING=180
#                         SPEED=3.0
#
#   3. JSON               {"lat": -22.878029, "lon": -43.155934,
#                          "heading": 180, "speed": 3.0}
#
#   4. NMEA 0183          $GPRMC,123519,A,2252.682,S,04309.356,W,3.0,180.0,230925,,,A*XX
#                         $GPGGA,123519,2252.682,S,04309.356,W,1,08,0.9,3.0,M,,,,*XX
#
# Course is degrees true, speed is knots; both are optional. With NMEA, the
# last valid RMC or GGA sentence wins, so appending to a log works.
#
# Write to a temporary file and rename, so the scope never reads a half-written
# file:
#   while :; do gpspipe -w -n 5 | grep -m1 RMC > POSITION.tmp \
#               && mv POSITION.tmp POSITION; sleep 1; done
#
# For testing without a GPS, tools/ship-sim.js takes a vessel up and down the
# main channel of Guanabara Bay at 8 knots and rewrites this file each second:
#   node tools/ship-sim.js
#
# Shipped values: Guanabara Bay, Rio de Janeiro, making 3 knots due south.

LAT=-22.878028696269414
LON=-43.15593411053736
HEADING=180
SPEED=3.0
